          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>In acute tubular necrosis, shed epithelial debris combines with Tamm-Horsfall pr</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>In acute tubular necrosis, shed epithelial debris combines with Tamm-Horsfall protein to form which characteristic urinary finding?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Waxy casts</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Waxy casts indicate chronic tubular atrophy and prolonged urinary stasis, not the acute necrosis and debris shedding seen in ATN.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Red blood cell casts</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Red blood cell casts indicate glomerular bleeding, as in glomerulonephritis. ATN is a tubular, not glomerular, injury.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Granular muddy-brown casts</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Necrotic tubular epithelial cells and cellular debris bind Tamm-Horsfall protein, forming granular muddy-brown casts — the hallmark urinary finding of acute tubular necrosis.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>White blood cell casts</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>White blood cell casts indicate pyelonephritis or acute tubulointerstitial nephritis with neutrophilic infiltration, not the non-inflammatory necrosis of ATN.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "The correct answer is granular muddy-brown casts. In ATN, necrotic epithelial debris binds Tamm-Horsfall protein, forming these distinctive casts that also obstruct tubular lumens and reduce GFR."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct. Granular muddy-brown casts are pathognomonic for ATN and contribute to one of the four mechanisms reducing GFR — tubular obstruction."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
