          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>Which urinary finding distinguishes acute pyelonephritis from lower urinary trac</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>Which urinary finding distinguishes acute pyelonephritis from lower urinary tract infection (cystitis)?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Pyuria</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Pyuria (white blood cells in urine) occurs in both cystitis and pyelonephritis. It indicates urinary tract inflammation but does not localise the infection to the renal parenchyma.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>White blood cell casts</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>White blood cell casts form within renal tubules and prove parenchymal infection. Their presence distinguishes pyelonephritis from simple bladder infection.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Bacteriuria</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Bacteriuria is present in both upper and lower urinary tract infections. A positive urine culture confirms infection but does not localise it to the kidney.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Nitrite-positive dipstick</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Nitrites indicate the presence of nitrate-reducing bacteria (typically Gram-negative organisms) in the urine but can be positive in both cystitis and pyelonephritis.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "The correct answer is white blood cell casts. Only casts form within renal tubules, proving parenchymal involvement. Pyuria, bacteriuria, and nitrites occur in both upper and lower urinary tract infections."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct. White blood cell casts can only form within the renal tubules, making them definitive evidence of renal parenchymal infection."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
                "enableRetry": true,
                "enableSolutionsButton": true,
