          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>A 30-year-old woman presents with microangiopathic haemolytic anaemia, thrombocy</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A 30-year-old woman presents with microangiopathic haemolytic anaemia, thrombocytopenia, acute kidney injury, fever, and confusion. Which laboratory finding would confirm thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura rather than haemolytic uraemic syndrome?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Schistocytes on peripheral blood film</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Schistocytes (fragmented red cells) are present in both TTP and HUS. They indicate microangiopathic haemolysis but do not distinguish between the two conditions.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Elevated serum lactate dehydrogenase</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Elevated LDH reflects haemolysis and tissue ischaemia, both of which occur in TTP and HUS alike. It supports thrombotic microangiopathy but does not differentiate the two.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Positive stool culture for E. coli O157:H7</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>A positive stool culture for Shiga toxin-producing E. coli would point towards haemolytic uraemic syndrome, not TTP. HUS typically follows bloody diarrhoea in children.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Severely reduced ADAMTS13 activity</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>An autoantibody against ADAMTS13 is the defining pathogenic mechanism of TTP. Severely reduced ADAMTS13 activity allows ultra-large von Willebrand factor multimers to accumulate, driving widespread platelet-rich thrombi. This finding confirms TTP and guides life-saving plasma exchange.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "The correct answer is severely reduced ADAMTS13 activity. Schistocytes and elevated LDH are shared features of all thrombotic microangiopathies. A positive E. coli O157:H7 culture would point towards HUS, not TTP."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct. ADAMTS13 deficiency is the hallmark of TTP. Plasma exchange removes the autoantibody and replenishes the enzyme — untreated TTP carries near-90% mortality."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
                "enableRetry": true,
