          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>Which genetic alteration drives the pathogenesis of clear cell renal cell carcin</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>Which genetic alteration drives the pathogenesis of clear cell renal cell carcinoma?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Trisomy of chromosomes 7 and 17</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Trisomies of chromosomes 7 and 17 are characteristic of papillary renal cell carcinoma, not the clear cell subtype.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Multiple chromosomal losses across several autosomes</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Multiple chromosomal losses characterise chromophobe renal cell carcinoma, which carries a better prognosis than clear cell RCC.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Loss of the WT1 tumour suppressor gene on chromosome 11p13</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>WT1 loss is associated with Wilms tumour (nephroblastoma), a childhood renal malignancy with triphasic histology, not with clear cell RCC in adults.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Biallelic loss of the Von Hippel-Lindau gene on chromosome 3p25</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Biallelic VHL loss lets HIF-1α accumulate, driving VEGF and erythropoietin upregulation. This fuels the angiogenesis and paraneoplastic features characteristic of clear cell RCC.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "The correct answer is biallelic VHL loss on chromosome 3p25. Each RCC subtype has a distinct genetic signature: trisomy 7 and 17 for papillary, multiple losses for chromophobe, and VHL loss for clear cell."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct. VHL loss on 3p25 is the driver event. The resulting HIF-1α accumulation explains both the tumour's rich chicken-wire vasculature and its paraneoplastic syndromes."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
