          "label": "<p>You examine a nephrectomy specimen showing a small, finely granular kidney. On m</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>You examine a nephrectomy specimen showing a small, finely granular kidney. On microscopy, you find hyaline thickening of the efferent arteriole. Which diagnosis does this finding establish?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Chronic glomerulonephritis</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Chronic glomerulonephritis does produce a small, finely granular kidney, but its microscopic hallmarks are glomerular scarring and fibrosis, not arteriolar hyalinosis.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Benign nephrosclerosis</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Benign nephrosclerosis involves hyaline arteriolosclerosis of the afferent arteriole, not the efferent. The afferent-versus-efferent distinction is critical at the bench.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Diabetic nephropathy</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Efferent arteriolar hyalinosis is pathognomonic for diabetic nephropathy. This single microscopic finding resolves the differential among multiple conditions sharing a finely granular kidney on gross examination.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Chronic pyelonephritis</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Chronic pyelonephritis produces coarse cortical scars overlying blunted, deformed calyces — a distinctly different gross pattern from the finely granular surface seen here.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "The correct answer is diabetic nephropathy. Efferent arteriolar hyalinosis is pathognomonic for diabetes. Benign nephrosclerosis affects the afferent arteriole — that distinction is the key to the bench diagnosis."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct. While benign nephrosclerosis, diabetic nephropathy, and chronic glomerulonephritis all share a small, finely granular kidney, efferent arteriolar hyalinosis is pathognomonic for diabetes — resolving the diagnosis in under two minutes."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
